{"title":"Career Roadmap · NGO Nutritionist","role":"NGO Nutritionist","summary":"An NGO Nutritionist works with nonprofit organizations, community programs and development agencies to improve nutrition and health outcomes among specific populations.","facts":{"Current degree":"Allied Health Sciences (B.Sc / BPT / BOT / BMLT etc.)","Course":"Public Health Nutrition / Community Nutrition"},"about":[],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Career Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An NGO Nutritionist works with nonprofit organizations, community programs and development agencies to improve nutrition and health outcomes among specific populations."},{"type":"p","text":"The role combines nutrition science, community health, program implementation, education, fieldwork, monitoring and data analysis."},{"type":"p","text":"Typical focus areas include:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Maternal nutrition"},{"text":"Infant and young-child nutrition"},{"text":"Adolescent nutrition"},{"text":"Child malnutrition"},{"text":"Anaemia prevention"},{"text":"Community nutrition education"},{"text":"School nutrition"},{"text":"Food security"},{"text":"Micronutrient programs"},{"text":"Public-health campaigns"},{"text":"Nutrition screening and referral"}]},{"type":"p","text":"NGO Nutritionists may work with:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Rural communities"},{"text":"Urban underserved populations"},{"text":"Schools"},{"text":"Women and children"},{"text":"Tribal communities"},{"text":"Older adults"},{"text":"Vulnerable households"},{"text":"Community health workers"}]},{"type":"note","label":"Qualification note","text":"“NGO Nutritionist” is an employment role rather than a single standardized professional license. Eligibility varies by NGO and project. A recognized nutrition/public-health qualification is strongly preferred, while some organizations recruit candidates from related health-science backgrounds for program or field roles."}]},{"heading":